   "l": "A man in his living: soft and weak.\nIn his dying: hard and strong.\nThe ten-thousand things, the grasses and trees, in their living: soft and tender.\nIn their dying: withered and dry.\nTherefore the hard-and-strong are death's company,\nthe soft-and-weak are life's company.\nAnd so: arms that are strong — then they do not prevail,",
   "v": true,
   "i": "A man alive is soft and yielding; dead, he is rigid and hard. The ten thousand things, the grasses and the trees, are soft and tender while they live; dead, they are withered and dry. So the rigid and hard keep company with death, and the soft and yielding keep company with life. And so an army that is strong does not win,",
   "n": "The chapter is built almost entirely out of paired adjectives — 柔弱, 堅強, 柔脆, 枯槁, and 強大 at the end — each of them a coupled word rather than two descriptions, and each used as a thing rather than as a quality: 'the hard-and-strong', not 'things that are hard and strong'.\n\n也 is doing a job it has lost in modern Chinese. Here it is not 'also' but a pause: 人之生也 sets up the topic — 'as for a man's being alive' — and holds it a beat before the comment drops. Note that no verb 'to be' ever arrives; 柔弱 simply follows, and English has to supply the 'is'.\n\n徒 is worth a look. The character is a man going on foot, and the word is a company travelling together — a following, a party, the people who belong to one side. 死之徒 is not 'the dead' but 'death's party': those who have thrown in with it.\n\nWang Bi reads the last line as a plain political observation: to hold arms strong and bully all under heaven with them is what everything hates, and so such a one necessarily fails to win. The reason is in the four lines above it — an army that is rigid is already in death's company.",
   "p": true
  },
