   "n": "One of the book's real cruxes, and the reader should know it. The received Wang Bi text prints 兵, 'weapons', which reads oddly after 兵強 in the line before it — and Wang Bi's whole comment on it is four characters, 物所加也, 'it is what things are set upon', using the same 加 that chapter 69 used for two armies matched against each other. On his reading the strong tree is simply what the axe is brought to: grow thick and you are timber.\n\nOther transmitted texts read 折 zhé, 'breaks', and 共 (taken as 拱, 'a span in girth' — big enough to fell); the Mawangdui manuscripts differ here again. The original wording is not recoverable. We print what Wang Bi read and translate it as he glossed it, and the alternatives stand on the record."
