   "i": "and what is soft and yielding takes the place above.",
   "n": "Follow Wang Bi and the image that has been running since the second line finally shows itself: the heavy dead wood is down at the root, the green and bending stuff is up in the light, and 上 and 下 are literal positions in a living thing rather than ranks.\n\nRead without him, the couplet is a ranking after all — the great and strong end up underneath, the yielding on top — which is the more usual reading and squares with the rest of the book. The two are not far apart, and nothing in the six characters decides between them.\n\nEither way, notice that this is the one place the book reverses its own habit. Chapter 66 praised the sea for lying below; here below is where the dead wood is. The preference is not for low ground as such but for what is still growing."
